          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c is 58.",
          "answer": "An HbA1c of 58 mmol/mol (about 7.5%) is above the prediabetes range. The guidance I could access, the diabetes association standards summary, describes prediabetes as HbA1c 5.7 to 6.4%. Values below the diagnostic thresholds fall in that range, so 7.5% sits above it. The same summary describes an HbA1c below 7% as a reasonable goal for many non-pregnant adults. Your value is above that goal. Source: *Type 2 diabetes: who is screened, and the diagnostic thresholds*; *Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals*.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not state the exact diagnostic cut-off for diabetes, so I can't tell you from them whether your result is in the diabetes range. Nor do they say how a single reading is confirmed. They do say that when more than one test is done and only one is abnormal, the usual approach is to repeat the abnormal one. I could not retrieve a passage explaining what HbA1c measures over time, so I can't explain that from the sources.\n\nA single result is one measurement. Whether it establishes a diagnosis depends on the full clinical picture and on repeat testing, which only a clinician can interpret for you. Please take this result to your doctor or diabetes care team. Ask them what the result means for you, whether it needs repeating, and what the next steps are. This is general information, not a diagnosis.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
